WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:06.240
Another way to delete all the information within&nbsp;
a table is to run a TRUNCATE TABLE statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.560
with the table name. With DELETE, you can specify&nbsp;
criteria to use for what is deleted from a table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.880
With TRUNCATE TABLE, the table is emptied,&nbsp;
as there are no criteria to be specified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:31.040
Also, note that a table with a one-to-many&nbsp;
relationship with another table will, by default,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.640
have the records from the many table deleted&nbsp;
if any records from the one table are deleted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:46.000
To illustrate this, I am going to show some&nbsp;
records from the Sales.SalesOrderHeader table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.640
This table has a one-to-many relationship&nbsp;
with the Sales.SalesOrderDetail table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:05.600
If I were to delete specific records from this&nbsp;
table, any records with the same sales order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.080
numbers in the SalesOrderDetail&nbsp;
table would be deleted as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:19.200
For this reason, it is so important to be&nbsp;
careful with any deletes from any tables.

